<ConverterPage
  path="/shusha-to-unicode"
  lede="Paste Shusha font Hindi on the left and get Unicode on the right, once the Shusha layout has been mapped."
>
  <h2>About Shusha</h2>
  <p>
    Shusha is an older Hindi DTP font from the same era as Kruti Dev and
    Chanakya, and it turns up in scanned office documents and in files handed
    down through several machines. Like the others it is a font, not an
    encoding: the Hindi is stored as ASCII and only looks like Hindi when the
    font is installed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Its key arrangement is its own, so nothing from the Kruti Dev map carries
    over. Until it has been read off real Shusha documents this page has no
    converter on it. Try <a href="/">Kruti Dev to Unicode</a> if your document
    turns out to be Kruti Dev after all.
  </p>
</ConverterPage>
